// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`the cards of lessons 1 to 4 against docs/academy/lessons.md > l1_sun: every learner-visible string is pinned, so a change is a deliberate update 1`] = `
[
  "l1_sun",
  "The [[sub_solar_point|sub-solar point]] is the place on me where the Sun is straight overhead. TrueSouth asks JPL Horizons for it at every hour of the time grid (science notes, section 3.1). My equator is tilted only a little to my path around the Sun (science notes, section 3.4), so that point always stays near my equator, and seen from my south pole the Sun sits near the horizon all year. As I turn, the point goes round my equator, so the Sun circles Pip's horizon instead of climbing.",
  "one-line",
  "A site's Sun [[elevation]] follows from the sub-solar point alone:",
  "\\sin e = \\sin\\varphi\\sin\\varphi_S + \\cos\\varphi\\cos\\varphi_S\\cos(\\lambda - \\lambda_S)",
  "block",
  "The sine of the Sun's elevation e is: the sine of the site's latitude times the sine of the sub-solar latitude, plus the cosine of the site's latitude times the cosine of the sub-solar latitude times the cosine of the gap between their east longitudes.",
  "14",
  "System view coordinates",
  "e",
  "The Sun's [[elevation]] at the site",
  "rad",
  "\\varphi",
  "The site's [[latitude]]",
  "rad",
  "\\varphi_S",
  "The sub-solar point's latitude",
  "rad",
  "\\lambda",
  "The site's [[east_longitude|east longitude]]",
  "rad",
  "\\lambda_S",
  "The sub-solar point's east longitude",
  "rad",
  "highest",
  "A rule of thumb for the most the Sun's centre can rise, in the best season, on a smooth sphere. It ignores the terrain, the observer's height and the Sun's disc:",
  "E_{max} \\approx 90° - |\\varphi| + 1.54°",
  "block",
  "The highest the Sun gets is about 90 degrees, minus how far the site is from the equator, plus the tilt of my equator to my path around the Sun.",
  "3.4",
  "Two rules of thumb the Academy quotes",
  "E_{max}",
  "The most the Sun's centre can rise above level, in the best season",
  "°",
  "\\varphi",
  "The site's [[latitude]]",
  "°",
  "direction",
  "The exact direction, which TrueSouth stores, starts from Pip's own position, not from my centre:",
  "\\hat d = \\frac{\\vec b - \\vec r}{|\\vec b - \\vec r|}",
  "block",
  "The direction d is the Sun's position b take away Pip's position r, divided by the length of that difference.",
  "3.2",
  "From the global pull to any site",
  "\\hat d",
  "The direction from Pip to the Sun, of length one",
  "\\vec b",
  "The Sun's position, measured from my centre",
  "m",
  "\\vec r",
  "Pip's position, measured from my centre",
  "m",
  "one hour at Pip's site, at {{example.l1.example_utc}} UTC",
  "Connecting Ridge, at the hour when the Sun is highest in the story month.",
  "Pip's latitude, $\\varphi$",
  "{{example.l1.site_lat_deg}}°",
  "The sub-solar latitude, $\\varphi_S$",
  "{{example.l1.example_subsolar_lat}}°",
  "The sub-solar east longitude, $\\lambda_S$",
  "{{example.l1.example_subsolar_lon}}°",
  "Putting them into the one-line formula",
  "\\sin e = \\sin({{example.l1.site_lat_deg}}^\\circ)\\sin({{example.l1.example_subsolar_lat}}^\\circ) + \\cos({{example.l1.site_lat_deg}}^\\circ)\\cos({{example.l1.example_subsolar_lat}}^\\circ)\\cos(\\lambda - {{example.l1.example_subsolar_lon}}^\\circ)",
  "The rule of thumb at Pip's latitude, for the best season",
  "E_{max} \\approx 90° - |{{example.l1.site_lat_deg}}°| + 1.54°",
  "The Sun's [[azimuth]], as stored",
  "{{example.l1.example_sun_az}}°",
  "The Sun's elevation, as stored, $e$",
  "{{example.l1.example_sun_el}}°",
  "The one-line formula gives about the same height. The stored numbers come from the exact direction, which also counts how high Pip stands above my centre.",
  "topocentric",
  "science/src/truesouth/ephemeris.py",
  "Turns the sub-solar point into the Sun's direction from any site.",
  "sunElevationDeg",
  "web/src/lib/geo/system.ts",
  "The one-line formula, used to light the System view.",
  "Mixing east and west longitudes. TrueSouth writes [[east_longitude|east longitude]] everywhere, from 0 to 360; a west longitude in its place puts the Sun on the wrong side of Pip's sky.",
  "Assuming my pole has half a month of light, then half a month of dark, as my equator does. Here the Sun circles the horizon, so light or shadow depends on the terrain in each direction. That is lesson 2.",
]
`;

exports[`the cards of lessons 1 to 4 against docs/academy/lessons.md > l2_skyline: every learner-visible string is pinned, so a change is a deliberate update 1`] = `
[
  "l2_skyline",
  "With the Sun this low, the terrain decides. In each direction the [[skyline]] is the highest angle of any terrain that way, and Pip is in sunlight only while the Sun clears it. The Sun is a disc, not a point, so it can be partly hidden: the [[disc_fraction|disc fraction]] says how much of it shows.",
  "elevation",
  "How high a piece of terrain looks, at a ground distance $s$. Leave out the drop $s^2/2R$ and this is the flat version from the lesson: height above you, over distance.",
  "e \\approx \\arctan\\left(\\frac{(z - z_0 - h_{obs}) - \\dfrac{s^2}{2R}}{s}\\right)",
  "block",
  "The elevation e is about the angle whose tangent is: the terrain's height above your eyes, take away the drop, all divided by the distance.",
  "4",
  "How high a piece of terrain looks",
  "e",
  "The terrain's [[elevation]]: how far above level it looks",
  "rad",
  "z",
  "The terrain point's height above my reference sphere",
  "m",
  "z_0",
  "The ground's height under the observer",
  "m",
  "h_{obs}",
  "How high the observer's eyes are above the ground",
  "m",
  "s",
  "Distance along the ground to the terrain point",
  "m",
  "R",
  "My radius, the size of my reference sphere",
  "m",
  "switch",
  "The skyline is built two ways. Close to Pip, rays are marched out along each [[skyline_bin|skyline bin]]; beyond the switch distance, every map pixel is visited, so no peak can slip between two rays:",
  "s_{switch} = \\frac{\\text{pixel size}}{\\tan 0.25°} \\approx 229 \\times \\text{pixel size}",
  "block",
  "The switch distance is the pixel size divided by the tangent of a quarter of a degree: about 229 times the pixel size.",
  "5",
  "The skyline",
  "s_{switch}",
  "The distance where one map pixel is as wide as one skyline bin",
  "m",
  "disc",
  "How much of a disc shows: cut it into thin upright strips, and add up the part of each strip that stands above the skyline $H$.",
  "f = \\frac{\\sum_i \\operatorname{clamp}\\big(e_c + c_i - H(A_i),\\ 0,\\ 2c_i\\big)}{\\sum_i 2c_i}",
  "block",
  "The disc fraction f is the sum over the strips of the part of each strip above the skyline, kept between zero and the strip's full height, divided by the sum of the strips' full heights.",
  "6",
  "How much of a disc is visible",
  "f",
  "The [[disc_fraction|disc fraction]]: 0 fully hidden, 1 fully clear",
  "e_c",
  "The elevation of the disc's centre",
  "rad",
  "c_i",
  "Half the height of strip i",
  "rad",
  "H(A_i)",
  "The skyline's elevation at strip i's azimuth",
  "rad",
  "the hour from step 1, at {{example.l2.site_name}}",
  "At {{example.l2.example_utc}} UTC. The skyline in the Sun's direction is one [[skyline_bin|skyline bin]], set by its [[blocker]].",
  "The Sun's [[azimuth]]",
  "{{example.l2.sun_az_deg}}°",
  "The Sun's elevation, $e_c$",
  "{{example.l2.sun_el_deg}}°",
  "The blocker's distance along the ground, $s$",
  "{{example.l2.ridge_dist_km}} km",
  "The blocker's height above Pip's eyes, $z - z_0 - h_{obs}$",
  "{{example.l2.ridge_rise_m}} m",
  "The flat version, for comparison",
  "\\arctan\\left(\\frac{ {{example.l2.ridge_rise_m}}\\,\\text{m} }{ {{example.l2.ridge_dist_km}}\\,\\text{km} }\\right)",
  "The skyline in that bin, on the round Moon, $H$",
  "{{example.l2.ridge_el_deg}}°",
  "The share of the Sun's disc that shows, $f$",
  "{{example.l2.sun_frac}}",
  "The Sun's centre is {{compare:l2.sun_el_deg,l2.ridge_el_deg|below|above|level with}} the skyline: {{example.l2.sun_el_deg}}° against {{example.l2.ridge_el_deg}}°.",
  "elevation_angle",
  "science/src/truesouth/skyline.py",
  "Works out the exact angle for one terrain point, with the round Moon in it.",
  "compute_skyline",
  "science/src/truesouth/skyline.py",
  "Builds a site's skyline, near field and far field, and keeps each bin's blocker.",
  "visible_fraction",
  "science/src/truesouth/discs.py",
  "Cuts the disc into strips and adds up the part above the skyline.",
  "A peak slipping between two rays. Far from Pip, neighbouring rays are far apart, so a narrow peak can fall between them. That is why the far field visits every map pixel instead.",
  "Treating the Sun as a dot. With its centre just below the skyline its top can still shine, and with its centre just above, part of it is hidden. TrueSouth counts the share that shows.",
  "Measuring a ridge's height above a reference instead of above Pip's eyes. Only $z - z_0 - h_{obs}$ sets the angle.",
]
`;

exports[`the cards of lessons 1 to 4 against docs/academy/lessons.md > l3_horizon: every learner-visible string is pinned, so a change is a deliberate update 1`] = `
[
  "l3_horizon",
  "I am round, so the ground falls away below a level line as you look farther out. Far terrain sinks towards the horizon, and far enough away even a tall mountain drops out of sight. With the Sun this low, that small drop can decide light or shadow.",
  "exact",
  "Exactly: an observer at radius $R_0$ looks at a terrain point at radius $R_1$, a ground distance $s$ away, with $\\gamma = s/R$.",
  "\\tan e = \\frac{R_1\\cos\\gamma - R_0}{R_1\\sin\\gamma}",
  "block",
  "The tangent of the elevation e is: R one times the cosine of gamma, take away R nought, all divided by R one times the sine of gamma.",
  "4",
  "How high a piece of terrain looks",
  "e",
  "The terrain's [[elevation]]: how far above level it looks",
  "rad",
  "R_1",
  "The terrain point's distance from my centre",
  "m",
  "R_0",
  "The observer's eyes' distance from my centre",
  "m",
  "\\gamma",
  "The ground distance as an angle at my centre",
  "rad",
  "elevation",
  "When $s$ is much smaller than $R$, the exact formula becomes the simple one, with the drop $s^2/2R$ in it:",
  "e \\approx \\arctan\\left(\\frac{(z - z_0 - h_{obs}) - \\dfrac{s^2}{2R}}{s}\\right)",
  "block",
  "The elevation e is about the angle whose tangent is: the terrain's height above your eyes, take away the drop, all divided by the distance.",
  "4",
  "How high a piece of terrain looks",
  "e",
  "The terrain's [[elevation]]: how far above level it looks",
  "rad",
  "z",
  "The terrain point's height above my reference sphere",
  "m",
  "z_0",
  "The ground's height under the observer",
  "m",
  "h_{obs}",
  "How high the observer's eyes are above the ground",
  "m",
  "s",
  "Distance along the ground to the terrain point",
  "m",
  "R",
  "My radius, the size of my reference sphere",
  "m",
  "dip",
  "Even flat ground has its horizon a little below level, for an observer at height $h$:",
  "-\\arccos\\left(\\tfrac{R}{R+h}\\right) \\approx -\\sqrt{2h/R}",
  "block",
  "Minus the angle whose cosine is R divided by R plus h. That is close to minus the square root of two h divided by R.",
  "4",
  "How high a piece of terrain looks",
  "h",
  "A height: the observer's above flat ground (the dip), or a peak's above the observer (the far limit)",
  "m",
  "far",
  "A peak $h$ above the observer sinks below the horizon beyond",
  "\\sqrt{2Rh}",
  "inline",
  "the square root of two times R times h.",
  "5",
  "The skyline",
  "h",
  "A height: the observer's above flat ground (the dip), or a peak's above the observer (the far limit)",
  "m",
  "the most distant blocker, at azimuth {{example.l3.example_bin_az}}°",
  "One [[skyline_bin|skyline bin]] of Connecting Ridge's skyline: the one whose [[blocker]] is farthest from Pip.",
  "Distance along the ground, $s$",
  "{{example.l3.example_dist_km}} km",
  "Height above Pip's eyes, $z - z_0 - h_{obs}$",
  "{{example.l3.example_height_m}} m",
  "Putting them into the simple formula",
  "e \\approx \\arctan\\left(\\frac{ {{example.l3.example_height_m}}\\,\\text{m} - \\dfrac{s^2}{2R} }{s}\\right), \\quad s = {{example.l3.example_dist_km}}\\,\\text{km}",
  "It would look, on a flat Moon",
  "{{example.l3.example_el_flat_deg}}°",
  "It looks, on the round Moon, $e$",
  "{{example.l3.example_el_true_deg}}°",
  "For scale: the drop at 20 km",
  "{{example.l3.drop_at_20km_m}} m",
  "In the real data the height above Pip is negative. Pip stands on a high ridge, so this far ground lies below Pip's eyes; it still sets the skyline here because nothing in that direction looks higher. On the round Moon it looks {{compare:l3.example_el_true_deg,l3.example_el_flat_deg|lower than|higher than|as high as}} on a flat Moon: {{example.l3.example_el_true_deg}}° against {{example.l3.example_el_flat_deg}}°.",
  "elevation_angle",
  "science/src/truesouth/skyline.py",
  "Works out the exact angle for one terrain point, with the round Moon in it.",
  "compute_skyline",
  "science/src/truesouth/skyline.py",
  "Looks out from the site in every direction and keeps the highest angle in each bin.",
  "A flat-Moon calculation, with no $s^2/2R$ term, puts far ridges in the sky that are really below the horizon.",
  "Using the simple formula at long range. It drifts as the distance grows, so the code uses the exact one from the same section.",
  "Mixing kilometres and metres: $s$ and $R$ must be in the same unit.",
  "l3.example_el_flat_deg",
  "l3.example_el_true_deg",
]
`;

exports[`the cards of lessons 1 to 4 against docs/academy/lessons.md > l4_link: every learner-visible string is pinned, so a change is a deliberate update 1`] = `
[
  "l4_link",
  "Earth hangs almost still over the middle of my near side, and my south pole sits at the edge of that face. My slow wobble, [[libration]], makes the [[sub_earth_point|sub-Earth point]] wander, so from Pip Earth bobs above and below the horizon each month. Pip can call home only while Earth clears the skyline by the profile's [[antenna_mask|antenna mask]].",
  "direction",
  "Earth's direction from Pip: Earth's position above the sub-Earth point, take away Pip's own position.",
  "\\hat d = \\frac{\\vec b - \\vec r}{|\\vec b - \\vec r|}",
  "block",
  "The direction d is Earth's position b take away Pip's position r, divided by the length of that difference.",
  "3.2",
  "From the global pull to any site",
  "\\hat d",
  "The direction from Pip to Earth, of length one",
  "\\vec b",
  "Earth's position, measured from my centre",
  "m",
  "\\vec r",
  "Pip's position, measured from my centre",
  "m",
  "shift",
  "Pip stands on my surface, not at my centre, so Earth's direction from Pip differs from its direction from my centre by at most about",
  "\\arcsin\\left(\\frac{R}{D}\\right) \\approx 0.26°",
  "block",
  "The angle whose sine is my radius divided by Earth's distance: about a quarter of a degree. It is largest when Earth sits at the horizon, as it does for the polar sites.",
  "3.4",
  "Two rules of thumb the Academy quotes",
  "R",
  "My radius",
  "m",
  "D",
  "Earth's distance from me",
  "m",
  "radius",
  "How big Earth's disc looks, from its radius $R_b$ and its distance:",
  "\\rho_b = \\arcsin\\left(\\frac{R_b}{|\\vec b - \\vec r|}\\right)",
  "block",
  "The disc's angular radius rho is the angle whose sine is the body's radius divided by its distance from Pip.",
  "3.2",
  "From the global pull to any site",
  "\\rho_b",
  "How wide the disc looks, from its centre to its edge",
  "rad",
  "R_b",
  "The body's radius",
  "m",
  "one hour near the antenna mask, at {{example.l4.example_utc}} UTC",
  "Connecting Ridge, at the story month's hour whose Earth [[clearance]] is closest to Pip's antenna mask, which the IM-2 Athena profile tags as assumed.",
  "Earth's [[azimuth]]",
  "{{example.l4.example_earth_az}}°",
  "Earth's [[elevation]]",
  "{{example.l4.example_earth_el}}°",
  "Earth's clearance above the skyline",
  "{{example.l4.example_earth_clear}}°",
  "An hour is a [[link]] when (science notes, section 9)",
  "\`earth_clear ≥ antenna_mask_deg\`",
  "Link time over the story month, with no margin",
  "{{example.l4.link_pct_mask0}}%",
  "With a 1° margin",
  "{{example.l4.link_pct_mask1}}%",
  "With a 5° margin",
  "{{example.l4.link_pct_mask5}}%",
  "Clearance is measured from the skyline, not from the level line. Where the skyline sits below level, Earth can clear it by more than its own elevation.",
  "topocentric",
  "science/src/truesouth/ephemeris.py",
  "Turns the sub-Earth point and Earth's distance into Earth's direction from any site.",
  "hourFlags",
  "web/src/lib/scoring/scoring.ts",
  "Marks each hour lit or dark, and link or blackout, under the mission profile.",
  "Fixed thresholds copied from another latitude. A rule such as "Earth well above the horizon" suits Apollo 11's site, where Earth hangs high; at my pole Earth stays near the horizon, so the rule throws away sites that work.",
  "Measuring the mask from the wrong line. The antenna mask is clearance above the skyline; measured from the level line, it lets terrain block the signal or throws away hours that work.",
  "Judging the whole month. Pip needs a link during its own mission window, not for half of every month. That is lesson 7.",
]
`;
